WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:10.340
In autumn 2023, researchers ran an experiment with nearly 1,000 students at a high school in Turkey.

2
00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:21.170
One group could ask AI anything while practising; another group's AI gave only hints, not answers; and a third group used no AI.

3
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:28.680
While practising, the group that could ask AI anything improved their scores by 48%.

4
00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:38.430
But in the exam, with no AI allowed, they scored 17% lower than students who had never used AI.

5
00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:49.030
In the hints-only group, that negative effect was largely cancelled out. And the researchers found the students didn't notice they had learned less.

6
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:59.280
This is the final episode of the core series. C8: when not to use AI, and keeping your own learning going.

7
00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:05.110
It's not that AI is bad. It's that some abilities need to grow in you.

8
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:12.820
That study has limits too: one school, one subject, and short-term effects only.

9
00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:25.050
A survey of 319 knowledge workers found that the more confidence people had in AI, the less critical thinking they reported. But that's self-reported.

10
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:37.640
And in an EEG experiment, people who wrote essays with an AI model found it hardest to quote what they'd just written. But that's a preprint, with a small sample.

11
00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:45.590
So write the conclusion as: in this study. Don't say: AI makes people stupid.

12
00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:52.167
So when shouldn't you use AI? Ask yourself six questions.

13
00:01:52.717 --> 00:02:01.437
Am I learning this skill? Does it need my judgement and responsibility? Is it something between people?

14
00:02:01.787 --> 00:02:10.947
Do the rules allow it? Does it involve privacy or confidentiality? Does the result matter, and I can't check it?

15
00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:20.497
If you're learning a skill, do it yourself first. If you need help, have AI give hints, not answers.

16
00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:30.827
Tool one: the AI-off checkpoint. Every so often, switch AI off, and do the same kind of task yourself.

17
00:02:31.177 --> 00:02:42.017
Here's a demo. A university student writes a lab report with AI switched off. She can describe the data, but can't explain what the results mean.

18
00:02:42.367 --> 00:02:51.767
So she changes how she uses it: she writes three key points herself first, then has AI only point out gaps in the logic.

19
00:02:52.117 --> 00:03:09.157
Tool two: the reflection log. After each use of AI, write five lines: what you did, which part was yours, could you do it with AI off, what you learned, and what you'll do yourself next time.

20
00:03:09.934 --> 00:03:13.934
Finally, let's tie all eight modules together.

21
00:03:14.284 --> 00:03:32.484
C1: tell talking apart from taking responsibility. C2: know why it makes things up. C3: brief tasks clearly. C4: check, according to risk.

22
00:03:32.834 --> 00:03:49.274
C5: guard three red lines. C6: test for bias, and think through your work. C7: set checkpoints and keep a supervision log. C8: know when to do it yourself.

23
00:03:49.624 --> 00:03:59.664
One thread runs through it: first checking, then supervising, and finally knowing when not to use it.

24
00:04:00.264 --> 00:04:15.704
Three badge levels. Level one, Know: pass practical quizzes. Level two, Use: hand in a task piece. Level three, Check: the checking challenge and a supervision log, plus an oral defence.

25
00:04:16.501 --> 00:04:23.461
Use it, check it, keep an eye on it, and know when to do it yourself.

26
00:04:24.061 --> 00:04:30.221
A small exercise: pick a skill, and set your first AI-off checkpoint.

27
00:04:30.571 --> 00:04:37.371
That's the end of the core series of AI Literacy for Everyone. Thank you for watching all the way through.
